<h2 id="types">Types are not erased</h2>
<p>TypeScript types are erased at run time; <code>JSON.parse</code> returns anything. Bayan types lay out the data: a <code>struct</code> is fixed fields held by value, and there is no structural typing, so two structs with the same fields are different types. There is no <code>any</code> or <code>object</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="TypeScript">type Role = "admin" | "guest";
interface User { name: string; role: Role; email?: string }

const a: User = { name: "aisha", role: "admin" };
const b = { ...a, name: "maryam", email: "m@example.com" };</code></pre>
<example name="guide-ts-struct"/>
<p><code>email</code> is <code>string?</code>: <code>null</code> unless set, unreadable until checked, and narrowed by <code>if b.email is not null { … }</code>, as TypeScript does. Creating <code>b</code> leaves <code>a</code> unchanged; <code>with</code> copies, because every struct is a value.</p>
<p>A struct literal must fill every field without a default; a missing field is BYN2109, not <code>undefined</code>.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `string`, found `null`
  --&gt; nulls.byn:2:24

  2 │     let name: string = null
    │                        ^^^^

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="unions">Tagged unions</h2>
<p>TypeScript hand-builds tagged unions with a <code>kind</code> field; Bayan has them as a type. A case may carry fields; a <code>switch</code> narrows to it, binds them, and the compiler checks every case is handled. The leading dot names a case of the expected type, one per line, <code>camelCase</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="TypeScript">type Outcome =
  | { kind: "ok"; value: string }
  | { kind: "failed"; reason: string }
  | { kind: "pending" };

function render(r: Outcome): string {
  switch (r.kind) {
    case "ok": return `ok: ${r.value}`;
    case "failed": return `failed: ${r.reason}`;
    case "pending": return "pending";
  }
}</code></pre>
<example name="guide-ts-union"/>
<p>The compiler always catches a missing case; TypeScript needs a <code>never</code> check. A taken name cannot be reused: <code>Result</code> is built in, so this union is <code>Outcome</code>.</p>

<h2 id="errors">Promises and exceptions become fails</h2>
<p>Nothing throws. A function that can fail lists its errors in the signature, so failure is in the type TypeScript's <code>throw</code> leaves out. The caller handles it with <code>catch</code> or passes it up with <code>try</code>; a <code>catch switch</code> covers every case.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="TypeScript">async function show(name: string): Promise&lt;string&gt; {
  try {
    return await load(name);
  } catch (e) {
    if (e instanceof NotFound) return "no such user";
    return "name required";
  }
}</code></pre>
<example name="guide-ts-errors"/>
<p>No <code>instanceof</code> and no catch-all hiding which error you got; see <a href="errors.html">Errors</a>. A case's doc comment is the text <code>$"{e}"</code> prints.</p>
<note>Planned: <code>async</code> and <code>await</code>. Unlike <code>Promise</code>, the function is not declared <code>async</code>, the call is marked, and a task cannot outlive the block that started it. The compiler rejects both keywords today; see the <a href="cookbook-concurrency.html">Concurrency cookbook</a>.</note>

<h2 id="functions">Functions and lambdas</h2>
<p>Parameter types come first: <code>i32 add(i32 a, by i32 b)</code>. Each later parameter carries a particle label, written at the call from a fixed list (<code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>as</code>); TypeScript uses an options object. A lambda takes its parameter types from the given function type, written without types.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="TypeScript">const applyTwice = (x: number, step: (n: number) =&gt; number) =&gt; step(step(x));
applyTwice(5, (n) =&gt; n * 3);</code></pre>
<example name="guide-ts-fn"/>
<p><code>it</code> is a one-parameter lambda's implicit parameter: <code>it * 3</code> is <code>x =&gt; x * 3</code>. A lambda must sit where its type is known; alone it has none, and the compiler says so:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2218:</span> `x =&gt; x + 1` needs a known type here
  --&gt; arrow.byn:2:15

  2 │     let inc = x =&gt; x + 1
    │               ^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write the function type it has, such as `let f: i64 -&gt; i64 = x =&gt; x`

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2218</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Write the type: <code>let inc: i64 -&gt; i64 = x =&gt; x + 1</code>.</p>

<h2 id="loops">map, filter and reduce</h2>
<p>Callback array methods are not in the compiler yet; a <code>for</code> loop does the same, with a postfix <code>if</code> or <code>continue if</code> to filter on one line, and <code>break</code> where TypeScript uses <code>.find</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="TypeScript">const total = prices.reduce((a, p) =&gt; a + p, 0);
const cheap = prices.filter((p) =&gt; p &lt; 10);
const doubled = prices.map((p) =&gt; p * 2);
const found = prices.find((p) =&gt; p &gt; 10);</code></pre>
<example name="guide-ts-loops"/>
<note>Planned: lazy <code>where</code>, <code>select</code> and <code>sumBy</code> on lists, written with <code>it</code> (<code>events.where(it.isValid)</code>); specified, BYN2003 today.</note>

<h2 id="surprises">What will surprise you</h2>

<h3 id="s-numbers">Numbers have a size</h3>
<p>TypeScript has one <code>number</code>; Bayan has only sized types: <code>i32</code>, <code>i64</code>, <code>u8</code>, <code>f64</code>. Integer division gives an integer (<code>7 / 2</code> is <code>3</code>), overflow panics, and lossy conversion is explicit: widening is implicit, but <code>i64</code> to <code>i32</code> needs <code>i32(a)</code>. <b>Why:</b> <code>number</code> hides a count from an id from a measurement, and has a 2<sup>53</sup> limit easy to hit.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `i32`, found `i64`
  --&gt; narrow.byn:3:18

  3 │     let b: i32 = a
    │                  ^
    │
  help: convert it if the value always fits; `i64` does not convert to `i32` without loss
    │ 3 │     let b: i32 = i32(a)
    │   │                  ++++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="s-equality">== compares like with like</h3>
<p>No <code>===</code>, no coercion: <code>1 == "1"</code> does not compile (BYN2208). A condition must be a <code>bool</code>; there is no truthiness, and <code>if n</code> on an integer is an error (BYN2202).</p>

<h3 id="s-null">Only one empty value</h3>
<p>There is <code>null</code>, spelled <code>?</code> in the type, and no <code>undefined</code>: <code>xs[9]</code> and <code>m["x"]</code> panic, and <code>tryGet(at:)</code> returns the optional.</p>

<h3 id="s-copy">Objects are values, lists are owned</h3>
<p>In TypeScript <code>const b = a</code> gives two names for one object. In Bayan a struct copies, and a <code>List</code> or <code>Map</code> that owns memory cannot be copied by accident:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `List&lt;i64&gt;` is unique: it owns its storage
  --&gt; copy.byn:3:13

  3 │     var b = a
    │             ^
    │
  help: hand the value over with `move a`; make an independent copy with `a.copy(to: alloc)`; pass
        it by `ref` to share it instead
    │ 3 │     var b = move a
    │   │             +++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2780</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Hand it over with <code>move a</code>, copy with <code>a.copy()</code>, or share with <code>ref</code>. <b>Why:</b> no garbage collector, so each list has one owner that frees it. See <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>

<h3 id="s-strings">Strings are UTF-8 bytes</h3>
<p><code>length</code> counts bytes, not UTF-16 units; <code>runeCount</code> counts characters. Slice by byte offset, <code>s[0..5]</code>; a cut through a character panics. There is no <code>+</code> on strings:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2209:</span> strings cannot be joined with `+`; use interpolation
  --&gt; concat.byn:3:11

  3 │     print(a + "y")
    │           ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write the parts as one interpolation: `$"{a}{b}"`, or `buf.append($"{a}{b}")` in a loop
    │ 3 │     print($"{a}y")
    │   │           +++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2209</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="s-classes">No classes, no this</h3>
<p>Methods live in a struct; there is no inheritance. A changing method is <code>ref this</code> and cannot be called on a <code>let</code>; <code>this</code> is an ordinary first parameter, so the call cannot change its meaning.</p>

<h3 id="s-modules">Packages and no runtime</h3>
<p>A directory is a module; <code>import app.orders</code> names it. No <code>node_modules</code>: dependencies are in <code>package.byn</code>, and the result is a native program. Decoding JSON into a typed struct is planned, not in the compiler yet.</p>
<p>Next: <a href="effective.html">Effective Bayan</a> says which form to choose.</p>
